    "title": "What are GC generations 0, 1, and 2?",
    "category": "4. Strings & Memory",
    "language": "csharp",
    "explanation": "The GC optimizes performance using an ephemeral generational model based on the premise that \"new objects die young\".\n- **Generation 0:** Contains short-lived, newly allocated objects. Collected frequently and extremely fast.\n- **Generation 1:** A buffer zone. Objects that survive a Gen 0 collection are promoted here. Collected occasionally.\n- **Generation 2:** Contains long-lived objects (like static data or singleton services). Collected rarely. A Gen 2 collection is a \"Full GC\" and is computationally expensive.",
    "example": "Temporary loop variables die in Gen 0. A localized caching service might make it to Gen 1. The main ASP.NET `DependencyInjection` container lives forever in Gen 2.",
    "code": "// Large Object Heap (LOH) is a special part of Gen 2.\n// Any object over 85,000 bytes goes straight to LOH/Gen 2.",
    "tip": "If your application experiences severe UI stuttering, it's often due to excessive Gen 2 collections caused by allocating too many Large Objects (like massive byte arrays).",
